# CLADE literature library

<!-- TEMPLATE — copied into a library repo as `readme.md` by the
     assemble-library skill, then rewritten for the clade. Replace CLADE
     throughout. The stats block below is regenerated by
     `python scripts/validate_bib.py --emit-readme`. -->

A corpus-ready library of the scientific literature on **CLADE**: the PDFs, a
bibliography that is a superset of them, a Darwin Core taxonomy snapshot, and
the configuration [corpus](https://github.com/caseywdunn/corpus) needs to build
a corpuscle from all of it.

<!-- BEGIN: stats (autogen by scripts/validate_bib.py --emit-readme) -->
<!-- END: stats -->

## What is here

| | |
|---|---|
| `library/` | The PDFs, sharded by first-author initial. `orphans/` holds files with no known bib entry |
| `CLADE.bib` | Every paper discovered. `file = {...}` only where a PDF exists — the rest is the **want-list** |
| `taxonomy.dwca.zip` | Darwin Core snapshot, committed so builds are offline and reproducible |
| `lexicon.yaml` | Terms to tag. **DRAFT — needs expert review** |
| `instructions.md` | Clade-specific guidance for the LLM. **DRAFT — needs expert review** |
| `scripts/` | The harvest and curation pipeline. Yours to edit |
| `build/` | Intermediate state. Gitignored and re-derivable — but see the warning below |

## The bib is a superset of the PDFs

Most retrieval attempts fail — expect a quarter to a third to succeed — because
most literature is not open access. That is the designed outcome, not a broken
build. Every paper discovered gets a bib entry whether or not a file was
obtained, so the ones without a `file` field are a **queryable want-list**: the
built corpus will answer "what are the most-cited papers I don't have?" from it.

To add a PDF by hand: drop it on the right shelf under the naming convention,
then re-run `python scripts/build_bib.py` to fill in its `file` field.

## Building the corpuscle

This directory *is* the corpuscle directory.

```bash
docker compose up -d grobid     # or however Grobid runs here; start it first
corpus check                    # config, toolchain, disk, models
corpus run                      # writes output/
corpus status --report
corpus serve                    # MCP server over the built corpuscle
```

## Open tasks

- [ ] **Review `lexicon.yaml`.** It is an agent draft. Whether it is any good is
      measurable only after a build — terms that matched nothing are the signal.
- [ ] **Review `instructions.md`.** Same.
- [ ] **Work the want-list.** The most-cited absent papers are the ones worth
      chasing with institutional access.

## Rebuilding the harvest

See [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) for the pipeline order and what each
script does.

> **`build/` is gitignored but not disposable.** `build/records.jsonl` costs a
> day of OpenAlex's free allowance to rebuild — the free tier is roughly 100
> search requests per day. Do not delete it casually.
